  "about": "How the folk of the human heartland name their settlements, in plain Common: a word for what the place lies by (a ford, a bridge, a mere, the fells, a wood, its fields) and a word that tells it from its neighbours (its trees, its beasts, its colour, its weather, its trade, the river it stands on): Whitefall, Thornford, Greenfield, Duskvale, Millbrook, Ashby. Read by CommonNames (src/world/common-names.ts). Each generic is used only where the settlement has what it needs ('when': its lie, a ford or bridge on its roads, a crossroads, its size, what made it as Foundings reads it, and for one there for its crossing, the very ford or bridge it is there for: 'crossed:ford'), and the more it tells of the place the likelier it is to be the name ('weight'): a ford or a harbour much more than the fields or the size. The land of the heartland (its rivers, lakes, woods, fens, moors, fells, peaks and passes) is named in Common too, by LandNames: its 'land' words say in Common what Deem's word for each kind of land is (a stream is a bourne, a grove a holt, a peak a pike). Kept out of the Deem tables so that Deem's draws do not move.",
